<td id="S5.F4.5.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.F4.5.1.9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;">63.8<span id="S5.F4.5.1.9.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">/</span>59.9</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure the importance of key components in our DiEP, we conducted ablation studies on Mixtral 8<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B with the following variants. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3.1 Effectiveness of Components ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DiEP: Adaptive Mixture-of-Experts Compression through Differentiable Expert Pruning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Row 1</span> serves as the baseline (NAEE), it only performs a layer-wise search for all possible expert combinations and has poor performance. <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Row 2</span> focuses on learning intra-layer expert importance <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to measure the global contribution of each expert. <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Row 3</span> denotes the variant of eliminating <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and applies inter-layer scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> to reweight expert activation frequencies as global importance scoring of each expert.
Compared with the baseline, the substantial performance gains demonstrate that the two components are both effective. To further investigate the efficiency of learnable inter-layer importance scoring, we replaced learnable <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed scores in <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Row 4-7</span>, where a ratio of 2:1 means <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2</annotation></semantics></math> for layers 1-16 and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> for layers 17-32. The results show that assigning higher scores to lower layers yields better performance in Mixtral 8<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B, but this method of artificially fixing parameters cannot be generalized to other SMoE architectures and could result in a significant waste of computational resources to identify the optimal <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, our method leverages complementary knowledge from intra-layer scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and inter-layer scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> for better expert selection, yielding superior performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.16105v1/vis_alpha_neurips.png" id="S5.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Intra-layer scores <math id="S5.F5.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> .</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16105v1/vis_beta0506.svg" id="S5.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inter-layer scores <math id="S5.F5.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of values distribution for intra-layer scores <math id="S5.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and inter-layer scores <math id="S5.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> on Mixtral 8<math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B when <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=50\%</annotation></semantics></math>.. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2 </span>Visualization Analysis for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the effectiveness of our proposed method, we visualized the variation of the updated intra-layer scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and inter-layer scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> after the pruning stage. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 5.3.1 Effectiveness of Components ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DiEP: Adaptive Mixture-of-Experts Compression through Differentiable Expert Pruning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, the distribution of intra-layer importance scores <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> reveals that experts in layers 1–15 tend to have higher average scores compared to those in layers 16–32. This suggests that shallower layers generally play a more significant role in the overall model.
Figure <a href="#S5.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 5.3.1 Effectiveness of Components ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DiEP: Adaptive Mixture-of-Experts Compression through Differentiable Expert Pruning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> illustrates the inter-layer importance scores, which corroborate the intra-layer observations. The overall trend indicates that the alternating update strategy effectively captures both intra- and inter-layer dependencies, ensuring that the MoE model retains critical information from shallower layers. Furthermore, a closer examination of layer 2 reveals that two experts. Specifically, the fourth and eighth experts exhibit markedly high <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values relative to their peers. This shows that these experts are consistently considered highly important by the model. Conversely, the remaining experts in layer 2 generally have low importance scores, indicating that the pruning strategy in this layer is not governed by a single importance criterion. Instead, it shows a clear preference for retaining these two experts through a global selection. Overall, these empirical findings further confirm the efficacy of our proposed differentiable expert pruning approach and underscore the synergistic relationship between <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.3 </span>Computation Cost Analysis</h4>

